  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1019 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nDry and breezy conditions are forecast for the short-term as an \nupper low scoots across the Four Corners. A trailing front will keep \nmoisture and storm chances just east of our area for Wed. Drier air \nunderneath the low will keep skies mainly clear with WSW winds of 10-\n20 mph, gusting to 30 mph Wed afternoon. Winds shift N and E during \nthe day on Thu as high pressure spills into the northern High \nPlains, slightly lower speeds than Wed. Temps remain near or just \nbelow normal under mostly sunny skies. \n\nA potent cold front arrives Thu evening from the NE, bringing strong \nE winds to the region Thu night into Fri. The strength of the sfc \nhigh behind the front is more in-line with what we tend to see in \nthe middle of winter (1045mb-ish). This will result in an impressive \npressure gradient across the CWA as the front sweeps through. West \nmtn slopes will see the gustiest winds, reaching 60 mph or higher\nfor peak gusts. Generally 25-35 mph sustained winds are modeled \nwith a pocket of lighter winds from Las Cruces up to Holloman AFB.\nNo changes are being made to the High Wind Watch that's in effect\nThu night-Friday afternoon. The watch will be upgraded to at \nleast an advisory at some point Wed with more advisories possible \nelsewhere. The strongest winds are expected to occur overnight Thu\ninto Fri morning with breezy winds lasting through Fri night. \nRecent rainfall should limit blowing dust concerns coming out of E\nNM and W TX, but can't be ruled out due to the high winds.\n\nA lobe of energy is modeled to break off from the upper low on Thu, \npulling moisture from the south into the area late this week. Low-\nmedium rain chances move in Thu night for far west TX, lasting \nthrough Fri. Isolated to scattered rain showers drift NW into Sat. \nHudspeth County is forecast to see the most rain with high \nuncertainty in rainfall amounts (0.25-0.5\" for NBM 50th %-ile \nthrough Fri, 1.5-2.5\" for 90th %-ile). Isolated flash flooding can\nbe seen, most likely in southern Hudspeth. Rain chances diminish \nas you head N and W. The much cooler temps may result in an early-\nseason rain/snow mix for the high terrain Sat AM if the moisture \nis advected far enough north around the upper low. Little to no \nsnow accumulation expected due to the warm ground temps. \n\nThe cut-off low off Baja should be picked up by a passing upper \ntrough well to the north on Sun, shoving most of the moisture \neastward, but leaving some over the Big Bend region. This remnant \nmoisture looks to be scooped up by the next Pacific low into \nmidweek, bringing more rain/storm chances to end the period. Low\nconfidence in impacts for this midweek system.\n\nTemperatures will crash to unseasonably cool levels (highs 15-25 \ndegrees below normal) behind Thu night's front. Highs struggle to \nreach 60F for the lowlands Fri and Sat with strong cold air \nadvection ongoing. It'll feel more like winter than early fall with \nsome spots expected to see their first freeze of the season Sat or \nSun AM as winds calm down. Temperatures rebound towards normal early \nnext week as SW flow develops.",
